[s0l0!-header][h1]Constants[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

A [b]constant [/b]stores a value that cannot be changed from its initial assignment. 
By using constants with meaningful names, code is easier to read and understand. 
To distinguish constants from variables, a common practice is to use uppercase identifiers.

One way to define a constant is by using the [b]const [/b]keyword in a variable declaration: [code format="c" codeId="1618"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  [b]const [/b]double PI = 3.14;
  printf("%f", PI);
    
  return 0;
}[/code]
The value of PI cannot be changed during program execution. 
For example, another assignment statement, such as PI = 3.141 will generate an error.
[note]Constants must be initialized with a value when declared.[/note]
Another way to define a constant is with the [b]#define[/b] preprocessor directive.
The [b]#define[/b] directive uses macros for defining constant values.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1635"]#include <stdio.h>

[b]#define PI 3.14[/b]

int main() {
  printf("%f", PI);
  return 0;
}[/code]
Before compilation, the preprocessor replaces every macro identifier in the code with its corresponding value from the directive. In this case, every occurrence of [b]PI [/b]is replaced with [b]3.14[/b].
The final code sent to the compiler will already have the constant values in place.

The difference between [b]const [/b]and [b]#define[/b] is that the former uses memory for storage and the latter does not.
[note]Do NOT put a semicolon character at the end of [b]#define[/b] statements. This is a common mistake.
We will learn more about [b]preprocessor directives[/b] in the next modules.[/note]

